{
  "dom_id": "step-q160429",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+2,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061c+2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that $|\\operatorname{sin}\u2061c|\\le 1$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points.",
